# frozen_string_literal: true

# A record's full activity history for users who are NOT auditors — the same
# timeline the auditor UI renders at /audit, built from the same published
# contract, without granting the auditor role.
#
# Generated by `rails generate audit_log:views:activity`. Yours to edit.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A LINK TO /audit. The auditor UI is a privileged
# surface: every record type, every actor, every action, plus retention and
# redaction tooling. `config.authorize` gates it, and that is correct. But "what
# happened to THIS record" is an ordinary operational question, and the person
# asking it should not need the keys to the whole log.
#
# So the split is by SCOPE, not by fidelity: same value objects, same detail, one
# record at a time, and only for record types this app is willing to show.
class ActivityController < ApplicationController
  # The gem's keyset pager, included rather than reimplemented.
  #
  # It is not a convenience. A keyset cursor is serialised with `to_json`, and
  # ActiveSupport renders a Time at millisecond precision while `occurred_at` is
  # `clock_timestamp()` — microseconds. A hand-rolled pager drops the trailing
  # digits, the next page's cursor names an instant just before its own row, and
  # rows vanish between pages. It presents as a rare flake, never as an error.
  # AuditLog::Pagination::FULL_PRECISION is the fix, and including this is how a
  # host app gets it.
  include AuditLog::Pagination

  # For audit_activity_visible? — ONE authorization rule, shared with the
  # show-page widget, so there is a single place to edit.
  include RecordActivity

  # A safe default, not an opinion about how you do authorization.
  #
  # It is doing TWO jobs, and they come apart:
  #
  #   1. Which record types have a readable history at all. If you already have
  #      Pundit, Action Policy, CanCanCan or your own policy layer, that decision
  #      probably belongs there instead — see #set_record below.
  #   2. Keeping `constantize` off a URL parameter. This one is not an
  #      authorization question and does not go away when you adopt a policy
  #      layer.
  #
  # Replace job 1 freely. Keep job 2 however you rewrite this.
  VIEWABLE = %w[<%= viewable.join(" ") %>].freeze

  PER_PAGE = 20

  before_action :require_activity_access
  before_action :set_record

  def show
    # .new rather than .for: `.for(record)` is sugar for when you hold the
    # record, and here we may not — a deleted order still has a history, and
    # that is exactly when somebody reads it.
    timeline = AuditLog::Timeline.new(
      record_type: @record_type, record_id: @record_id, range: bounded_range
    )

    @page       = paginate(timeline.activity_keys, limit: PER_PAGE)
    @activities = timeline.activities(@page.records)
    @timeline   = timeline
  end

  private

  # See RecordActivity#audit_activity_visible? — edit it there, not here, so the
  # show page and this page can never disagree about who may read a history.
  def require_activity_access
    return if audit_activity_visible?

    redirect_to main_app.root_path, alert: "You cannot view record history."
  end

  def set_record
    @record_type = params[:record_type]

    # SWAP THIS FOR YOUR OWN AUTHORIZATION if you have one. It is a placeholder
    # that fails closed, not a recommendation — this gem has no idea what you
    # use.
    #
    # What must survive the swap is the ORDER: something has to constrain
    # @record_type to a known set BEFORE the constantize below, because
    # `/<%= options[:path] %>/User/1` is a URL anyone can type and this page renders previous
    # values of audited columns. An allowlist is simply the smallest thing that
    # does it with no dependencies.
    #
    # A policy layer replaces the first half and not the second:
    #
    #   raise ActionController::RoutingError, "Not viewable" unless VIEWABLE.include?(@record_type)
    #   @record = @record_type.constantize.find_by(id: @record_id)
    #   authorize @record, :history?          # Pundit
    #   authorize! :history, @record          # CanCanCan
    #   authorize @record, to: :history?      # Action Policy
    #
    # Per-record rules ("this manager may read THIS order") only work once the
    # record is loaded, so they go after the find and not instead of the check.
    # A deleted record has no object to authorize — decide deliberately whether
    # its history stays readable.
    raise ActionController::RoutingError, "Not viewable" unless VIEWABLE.include?(@record_type)

    @record_id = params[:record_id]
    # find_by, not find: an audit trail outlives the record it describes, and a
    # deleted order's history is exactly when somebody needs to read it.
    @record = @record_type.constantize.find_by(id: @record_id)
  end

  # Unbounded by default, like the record screen in the auditor UI: one record
  # has bounded history. `?days=` is an opt-in fast path, and the view says which
  # one it ran — a narrowed query must never look like a complete one.
  def bounded_range
    days = params[:days].to_i
    return nil unless days.positive?

    days.clamp(1, 3_650).days.ago..Time.current
  end
end
